# Postmortem — OOMKilled Incident

**Duration:** 45 minutes
**Severity:** SEV-2
**Status:** Resolved

## Summary
The application went down for 45 minutes because it kept getting killed by `OOMKilled` — the container used more memory than its allowed limit, so the platform killed and restarted it over and over (crash loop), causing repeated failed requests for users.

## Timeline
| Time | Event |
|---|---|
| 00:00 | Memory usage starts climbing abnormally |
| 00:05 | First alert: memory usage > 90% |
| 00:07 | First OOMKilled event, container restarts automatically |
| 00:07–00:45 | Crash loop: container restarts, memory fills up again, gets killed again |
| 00:15 | On-call engineer starts investigating |
| 00:25 | Likely cause identified: memory leak or memory limit set too low |
| 00:35 | Temporary fix: increased memory limit, manually restarted service |
| 00:45 | Service stable, crash loop stopped |

## Root Cause
The container hit its memory limit and was killed by the OOM killer. Likely contributing factors:
- A memory leak in the app (objects/connections not being released)
- Memory limit set too low for real traffic load
- No auto-scaling, so all load stayed on one instance instead of spreading out
- No readiness checks, so traffic kept hitting the crashing container

## Recommendations

**Short-term**
- Raise the memory limit based on real usage data
- Add readiness/liveness probes so traffic avoids unhealthy containers
- Add an alert at 75% memory usage, not just at the OOM point

**Long-term**
- Profile the app to find and fix the memory leak
- Add auto-scaling based on memory usage (see autoscaling-policy.md)
- Set resource limits per service so one app can't use all cluster memory
- Write a runbook for OOMKilled incidents
